<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Salamis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/480-bce">480 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Salamis was a naval battle fought in 480 BCE in the straits between the island of Salamis and the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, during the second Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>. The allied Greek fleet, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, defeated the larger fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>, who then withdrew with much of his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The victory prepared the way for the Greek success at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis.jpg" alt="Battle of Salamis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Department of History, United States Military Academy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_salamis.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the urging of Themistocles the city then built a fleet of some 200 triremes, which formed the core of the Greek navy that defeated Xerxes I at the <b>Battle of Salamis</b> in 480 BCE, and Athenians fought at Plataea in 479 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of Salamis</b> in 480 BCE its navy played a significant role on the Persian side against the Greeks, and from the middle of the 5th century BCE warships were stamped on its coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the century after Cleisthenes its citizens beat the Persians at the Battle of Marathon in 490 BCE, helped to defeat them at the <b>Battle of Salamis</b> in 480 BCE, built the Parthenon and staged the dramas of Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Aegina gave earth and water to Persia in 491 BCE, but its ships won the prize for valour at <b>Salamis</b> in 480 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/aegina">Aegina</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Achaemenid Empire it was ruled by a Carian-Greek dynasty: Lygdamis I and his daughter Artemisia I, who commanded ships for Xerxes at <b>Salamis</b> in 480 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/halicarnassus">Halicarnassus</a></li><li><blockquote>The city sent ships to <b>Salamis</b> in 480 BCE and was usually a member of the Peloponnesian League.</blockquote><a href="/state/hermione">Hermione</a></li></ul></section>
